From c3bbf092e9c46a811de7c8fc1d506a9237c4dd66 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 09:43:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] lifecycle guard: see session-snapshot aliases; run
 startup-file proofs on CI (review 5888336995 nits 1-2)

Nit 2 (fail-open main did not have): the terminal backend sources a login
snapshot holding `alias -p` and `shopt -s expand_aliases` before every
command, so `alias t='time '` turns `t ( bash /x/huge.sh )` into a subshell
running an unscannable script, which the guard read as a mention.
- The snapshot bootstrap now prints the alias names it captured
  (`_alias_names_printf`), BaseEnvironment reports them through
  `lifecycle_guard_session_aliases()`, and gateway_lifecycle_block passes them
  to `scan_gateway_lifecycle(session_aliases=...)`.
- Any session alias (or unknown names: no snapshot yet, bootstrap silent)
  makes `aliases_possible` true for the whole walk; an alias that may rename a
  heredoc consumer, wrapper or shell (`alias python3=bash`) disables heredoc
  masking entirely.
- Shape corpus: new generator dimension "aliases defined outside the walked
  text" (6 snapshots x 3 forms x 5 payloads), run in real bash the way the
  backend does (source snapshot, eval command). A mutant guard that ignores the
  snapshot produces 21 corpus violations; this tree reports violations=0 on
  the full corpus (17410 cases).

Nit 1: the startup-file execution proofs no longer degrade to a warning the
parallel runner drops. `startup_shell_prefix()` finds a shell that sources
`$HOME` startup files (plain bash, else bubblewrap masking /etc/profile*,
which the CI test job installs); with one the proof must pass, without one
it is a real pytest skip whose reason is also appended to
$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY (run_tests.sh now forwards it).
